  "title": "The court cases deciding who gets inside the money system",
  "question": "Which cases determine master-account access, preemption, sovereign assets, agency survival, bank failure and the right to stand inside a payment system?",
  "bluf": "A map of 72 case families and 159 dated events on who can hold a Fed account, which agencies survive and how far emergency money powers reach. So far the courts mostly leave gatekeeping power with the Fed, Treasury and bank receivers.",
  "evidence_cutoff": "2026-10-13T20:30:00-04:00",
  "evidence_cutoff_note": "Targeted Supreme Court visual refresh through October 13 at 20:30 ET: the hub checks Custodia, Cook, Cantero, D.V.D. and National Park Service v. National Trust against their official Supreme Court dockets and their separately dated research owners. Kansas City's Custodia opposition was visible, with no separate Board filing, reply, distribution or Court disposition. The incorporated 72-case map body remains broadly bounded to July 11, and the separate July SVB trial outcome still requires a current court-record refresh.",
  "use_note": "Internal recall only for mapped case identities, holdings, procedural postures and money-perimeter issue families; the incorporated vault copy controls the wider map. The five-case hub is a focused current projection, not a whole-docket refresh. Do not treat advocacy as a finding, a stay as a merits judgment, cert-stage posture as final judgment, a future Payment Account as present access, or a case-specific holding as the complete boundary of Reserve-Bank discretion.",
